    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203175002952_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nThe Education Department seeks a part-time instructor committed to culturally responsive pedagogies, to teach EDUC 312: Integrating Arts in the Learning Process .   This course focuses on the integration of fine arts in the elementary school curriculum. Students will learn how to meaningfully incorporate the visual arts, drama, music, and dance across the natural sciences, social sciences, and humanities in K-8 classrooms to enrich the learning process. The course is held in person on Wednesday evenings from 7:00-9:50 PM during the Spring 2027 semester.  Qualifications :  A minimum of an earned master’s degree in education, fine arts, or related field(s).  Substantive experience teaching visual arts, drama, music, and/or dance in PK-12 settings or integrating fine arts into the self-contained elementary classroom.  Interested applicants should apply online at  Current Openings | Recruitment Please include a letter of interest and a CV. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The salary offered for this position is $6,120 per course and our benefits for part-time employees can be found at Benefits Overview for Part Time Employees  Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. A selective liberal arts college located on Chicago’s North Shore, Lake Forest College enrolls approximately 1,800 students from more than 40 states and from more than 100 countries. Lake Forest is one of the most diverse small colleges in the Midwest with approximately 50% of our student body comprised of domestic students of color and international students. If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203173651025_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Spanish",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nLake Forest College Department of Modern Languages and Literatures seeks a highly motivated tenure-track Assistant Professor of Spanish with expertise in Peninsular and/or Latin American Literatures and Cultures to begin August 2027. Areas of particular interest include, but are not limited to: Biopolitics, Gender/Sexuality/Queer Studies, and/or Sociolinguistics. We welcome candidates who incorporate comparative, interdisciplinary, and cultural studies approaches in their teaching and scholarly work. Ph.D. (or ABD status with Ph.D. completed by start date) in Spanish or Hispanic Studies is required. Candidates should possess outstanding skills in and enthusiasm for teaching all levels of language, literature, and culture. Candidate materials should show evidence of or potential for college-level teaching effectiveness, familiarity with education at a liberal arts institution, and evidence of scholarly productivity, commensurate with experience. Specific responsibilities include teaching six courses annually at all levels. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching excellence in a liberal arts setting that values interdisciplinary collaboration and diverse perspectives. Fluency in spoken, written and reading Spanish for effective undergraduate teaching and scholarly engagement is required. Candidates are expected to engage actively in departmental and campus-wide service, academic advising, and student-centered research, organizations, and programs. Candidates should possess outstanding skills in and enthusiasm for teaching in diverse classroom environments at all undergraduate college levels of language, literature, and culture. At Lake Forest College, the quality of a faculty member’s teaching is the most important criterion for evaluation. The College also expects peer-reviewed publications and active participation in the College community. To support faculty research, the college offers a paid semester-long research leave in the 4th or 5th year of service after a successful Third-Year Review. In a single .pdf, please upload to the Lake Forest College Career Center employment application portal: 1) a cover letter outlining your area of specialization, relevant teaching experience, and general research agenda; 2) a curriculum vitae ; 3) the names and contact information of three professional references; and 4) a statement of teaching philosophy, which should include, at a general level, your thoughts on teaching at a small liberal arts college with a diverse student body and, at a more specific level, ideas for courses that you might offer at Lake Forest College. All application materials should be uploaded to the portal or sent to  spanishsearch@lakeforest.edu by November 23,2026. Please address all communications to Search Committee Chair. Additional information, including letters of recommendation, evidence of teaching experience and effectiveness, and information on the candidate’s planned research agenda, will be requested of those chosen for first-round interviews. Lake Forest College offers comprehensive and competitive pay and benefits to attract and retain talented individuals and further our mission. The salary range for this position is $67,000 -$70,000. Benefit information can be found at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human- resources/employee-benefits. A selective liberal arts college located on Chicago’s North Shore, Lake Forest College enrolls approximately 1,800 students from more than 40 states and from more than 100 countries. Lake Forest is one of the most diverse small colleges in the Midwest with approximately 50% of our student body comprised of domestic students of color and international students. At Lake Forest College, the quality of a faculty member’s teaching is the most important criterion for evaluation. The College also expects peer-reviewed publications and active participation in the College community. If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203172703477_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Social Media Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nLake Forest College is seeking a Social Media Manager to join The Office of Communications and Marketing. Reporting to the Assistant Vice President of Communications and Marketing, the Social Media Manager leads Lake Forest College’s institutional social media presence, combining strategic oversight with hands-on content creation and execution. The position develops engaging, authentic, and data-informed social media strategies that strengthen the College’s brand, tell compelling stories, and build meaningful connections with key audiences. Responsibilities: Social Media Content Creation and Management: Create, capture, edit, publish, and manage engaging content across the College’s institutional social media channels, including photography and short-form video. Maintain a consistent and authentic College voice and ensure content supports institutional priorities, audience needs, and platform best practices. Social Media Strategy and Content Planning: Develop and execute channel-specific social media strategies and manage the College’s social media content calendar. Plan content around institutional priorities, departmental goals, events, campaigns, and timely opportunities while adapting strategies based on platform trends and evolving audience behaviors. Campus Collaboration and Story Development: Collaborate with OCM colleagues, campus partners, and students to identify compelling stories and content opportunities that bring the Lake Forest College experience to life. Coordinate content across channels and initiatives and serve as a resource to campus partners on social media strategy and best practices. Student Creator Program: Develop and manage a robust student creator program, working with campus partners to identify, mentor, and guide student content creators who can authentically capture and share the student experience across the College’s diverse campus community. Social Media Analytics and Optimization: Monitor and analyze social media performance, audience engagement, and channel trends. Translate performance data and audience insights into recommendations and adjustments that continually strengthen content strategy and effectiveness. Social Listening, Trends, and Issue Awareness: Stay closely attuned to conversations, trends, and activity across College social media channels to identify timely content opportunities and potential issues. Exercise sound judgment in recognizing sensitive conversations or concerns that should be elevated to College leadership. Event and Institutional Content Support: Provide social media and photography coverage for select College events and activities, including occasional coverage outside regular business hours, and assist with visual content needs that support broader OCM communications efforts. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and two (2) years of relevant professional experience in social media, digital communications, content marketing, or a related field. Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including writing, editing, and storytelling. Strong organizational and project management skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines. Ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, students, administrators, and external partners. Professional experience managing social media channels and developing digital content for an organization or brand. Demonstrated experience creating social-first content, including photography and short-form video. Experience developing and executing social media content calendars and campaigns. Experience using social media management, scheduling, analytics, and reporting tools. Working knowledge of major social platforms, including Instagram, Facebook, TikTok, LinkedIn, YouTube, and emerging platforms. Experience using photography, video editing, graphic design, or other digital content creation tools. Strong writing and editing skills with demonstrated ability to create engaging content for digital audiences. Ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines in a fast-paced, collaborative environment. Ability to work occasional evenings and weekends to support College events and social media or photography coverage.   Salary & Benefits Starting range is dependent upon individual qualifications and experience but expected to be in the range of $55,000 - $65,000. Benefits:  Employee Benefits | Lake Forest College Application  Interested applicants should apply online at  Career Center | Recruitment   If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit  Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203168996338_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Printing Services Operations Manager (Temporary)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nLake Forest College invites applicants for the part-time, non-exempt position of Printing Services Operations Manager. Reporting to the Assistant Director of Printing Services, the Printing Services Operations Manager is responsible for providing operational and administrative support for the Printing Services department in a fast-paced, detail-oriented environment.   This position is also responsible for supporting the daily operations to fulfill printing/copying production needs, managing inventory, billing, and supporting special projects. This is a part-time position, up to 20 hours per week, through May 31, 2027. May be extended based on performance, institutional need, and funding availability. Essential Responsibilities: Support the department’s day-to-day activities, including work orders, billing, and office organization. Understand PrintSmith billing system and support simple billing and invoicing. Monitor the ticketing system, post invoices for work orders, and create reports as needed. Maintain appropriate files, documents, and records for the department. Maintain excellent customer relations; field information requests and/or support calls from the College community and identify and facilitate a handoff to the appropriate resource.  Manage special projects and department events, including data collection, benchmarking, and survey response.  Effectively carry out other duties, as assigned. Required Qualifications: Associate’s degree and at least five years of high-level administrative experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Proficient with office computing hardware and software, including PCs and/or Apple computers, Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, and PowerPoint), etc. Project and time management skills, with ability to effectively manage competing priorities. Excellent verbal, written, interpersonal, and customer service skills.   Desired Qualifications: Experience in an academic environment. Experience with procurement/purchasing processes. Experience using Printing Services software applications and/or reporting tools.   Compensation and Benefits: Hourly Rate of Pay $20.60 Benefits information is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits Interested applicants should apply online at  Current Openings | Recruitment If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203168156950_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Dispatcher Public Safety",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nLake Forest College seeks a customer-focused and detail-oriented Public Safety Dispatcher for the night shift. The Dispatcher serves as the central communications hub for Lake Forest College’s Department of Public Safety. This position is responsible for receiving, prioritizing, and dispatching calls for service, monitoring alarms, and initiating emergency notifications. Dispatchers ensure timely, accurate, and professional communication between officers, campus partners, and external emergency responders to maintain a safe and secure campus environment. Key Responsibilities Call Handling & Dispatching: Receive emergency and non-emergency calls; prioritize and dispatch Public Safety Officers and other responders. Alarm Monitoring: Monitor intrusion, fire, panic, CCTV, and other safety systems; initiate appropriate response protocols. Emergency Coordination: Maintain communication with officers in the field; coordinate with local police, fire, and EMS when required. Information Management: Accurately document calls, incidents, and actions in the department’s records of management and dispatch systems. Campus Support: Provide directions, information, and assistance to students, staff, faculty, and visitors. Radio Operations: Operate two-way radios and communication systems with clear, professional etiquette. Confidentiality: Handle sensitive information with discretion and professionalism. Perform other administrative duties such as preparing reports as assigned. Working Conditions Must be available for day, evening, overnight, weekend, and holiday shifts to support 24/7 operations. Work is primarily indoors at a communications center; requires prolonged sitting, computer use, and focused attention.   Key Skills & Competencies Communication: Clear, calm, and professional verbal and written communication. Multitasking: Ability to manage multiple calls, radio traffic, and computer systems simultaneously. Decision-Making: Assess situations quickly and prioritize responses under pressure. Attention to Detail: Accurate data entry and documentation. Customer Service: Courteous and supportive interaction with the campus community. Requirements Education: High school diploma or equivalent work experience; Associate’s degree preferred. Experience: Prior dispatch, communications, or coordination experience preferred. Technical Skills: Proficiency with computer systems, records management software, Microsoft Office Suite, and multi-line phone systems. Availability: Ability to work rotating shifts and provide coverage as needed. Other key qualities: reliability, strong team orientation, ability to work under pressure, and commitment to campus safety and service. Physical Requirements Ability to remain seated for extended periods. Frequent use of hands for keyboarding and phone/radio operation. Visual and auditory acuity sufficient to monitor screens, alarms, and radio traffic. Compensation & Benefits Hourly rate: $18.50 - $20.00/hour (40 hour/week schedule) Benefits: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203168127935_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Computer Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nThe Department of Mathematics and Computer Science invites applications for a tenure-track position in Computer Science at the assistant professor level beginning Fall 2027. Candidates from all CS-related specialties are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to contribute to our Computer Science, Data Science, and Mathematics majors. We seek candidates with a strong commitment to teaching the Computer Science curriculum to a wide range of students, enthusiasm for teaching all levels of undergraduate students, and a research program that can involve undergraduate researchers. This position involves teaching three courses a semester. To support your research agenda, the college offers a paid semester-long research leave in the 4th or 5th year of service after a successful Third-Year Review. The majority of our STEM pool is female and also includes a significant number of first generation and traditionally underrepresented students. At Lake Forest College, the quality of a faculty member’s teaching is the most important. We are particularly interested in candidates committed to teaching a diverse student body.  Required Qualifications The position requires a Ph.D. in Computer Science or a computationally intensive field (to be completed no later than August 2027).  The candidate must be able to teach a wide range of undergraduate Computer Science courses. The candidate must be willing to teach a course in the College's first-year studies program The candidate must be able to advise Computer Science students and supervise research projects with undergraduates in Computer Science. The candidate must be able to maintain an active program of scholarly research.  Application Process  All completed applications received by November 22, 2026, will receive full consideration. A completed application includes the following documents: A current CV that includes documentation of teaching experience. A cover letter that describes your motivation for applying to Lake Forest College. Teaching Philosophy Statement: Include a statement about teaching practices in diverse and inclusive environments. A research plan that also details your strategy for involving undergraduates in your work. Three reference letters. All documents except for the reference letters should be uploaded through the Lake Forest College Career Center portal: Lake Forest College Career Center website   or sent electronically to Professor Enrique Treviño, cssearch@lakeforest.edu . Reference letters should be sent directly by email to cssearch@lakeforest.edu . Review of applications will begin promptly on November 22, 2026. About Lake Forest College Lake Forest College offers comprehensive and competitive pay and benefits to attract and retain talented individuals to further our mission. The salary range for this position is $77,000 - $82,000 and information on our benefits can be found at www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits . A selective liberal arts college located on Chicago’s North Shore, Lake Forest College enrolls approximately 1,800 students from more than 40 states and from more than 100 countries. Lake Forest is one of the most diverse small colleges in the Midwest with nearly 50% of our student body comprised of domestic students of color and international students. If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203165484267_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Coordinator for CARE",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nPosition Summary Lake Forest College invites applications for the full-time, exempt position of Coordinator for CARE (Community, Advocacy, Resources, and Empowerment). Reporting to the Director for CARE, the Coordinator serves as a key member of the CARE team by developing educational programs, advising student organizations, fostering student belonging, and supporting campus-wide initiatives that promote dialogue across difference and inclusive community. The Coordinator works collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and campus partners to create meaningful educational experiences that strengthen community, encourage engagement across difference, and support the success and well-being of all students. This position also assists with assessment efforts, campus climate initiatives, and the daily operations of the CARE Center. Job Responsibilities Student Engagement & Leadership Development Advise identity-based, culturally focused, and spiritual/religious/faith-based student organizations, supporting student leadership development, organizational effectiveness, and program planning. Support educational, social, and cultural programs that foster belonging, community engagement, and leadership development. Recruit, train, supervise, and mentor CARE student employees, interns, and student leaders. Collaborate with the Gates Center, Residence Life, New Student Orientation, and other campus partners to integrate CARE initiatives into the student experience. Coordinate pre-Orientation New Student program Dialogue Across Difference & Educational Programming Coordinate and facilitating educational workshops and dialogue programs focused on identity, communication, belonging, and constructive engagement across difference. Serve as a facilitator for campus conversations, dialogue series, and educational programs. Support implementation of dialogue across difference framework through co-curricular programming. Collaborate with faculty and staff to connect classroom learning with co-curricular experiences. CARE Center Operations Assist with the day-to-day operation of the CARE Center. Coordinate the Center's calendar, space utilization, student engagement initiatives, and signature programs. Coordinate communication efforts including newsletters, social media, website content, and promotional materials. Support budget tracking, purchasing, event logistics, and office administration. Campus Climate & Community Support Assist with campus climate initiatives including focus groups, surveys, and student feedback efforts. Monitor student concerns and emerging issues and communicate trends to the Director. Support restorative and educational responses following bias incidents or community concerns in collaboration with campus partners. Serve as a resource for students seeking support, referrals, or involvement opportunities. Assessment & Program Evaluation Collect participation data and assist in assessing CARE programs and initiatives. Utilize assessment results to recommend improvements to programs and services. Campus Collaboration Serve on institutional committees and working groups as assigned. Partner with Academic Affairs, Residence Life, Student Organizations, Athletics, Admissions, Human Resources, and other departments to promote an inclusive campus environment. Represent CARE during admissions programs, orientation, family weekends, and other institutional events. Required Skills & Qualifications Required Demonstrated experience working with diverse student populations in higher education or a related educational setting. Experience advising student organizations or facilitating student leadership development. Experience planning and implementing educational programs and campus events. Demonstrated ability to facilitate discussions involving identity, belonging, communication, and community. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Strong organizational and project management skills. Ability to collaborate effectively across departments. Ability to work independently while contributing to a collaborative team environment. Ability to work evenings and weekends as needed. Bachelor’s degree in related field Preferred Experience facilitating dialogue across difference or restorative dialogue. Experience supervising student employees or graduate assistants. Experience collecting or interpreting assessment data. Master's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, College Student Personnel, Counseling, Social Work, Education, or a closely related field required. Experience with student development theory, leadership education, or campus climate initiatives.Master's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, College Student Personnel, Counseling, Social Work, Education, or a closely related field required. Full benefits information including eligibility details is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203164807133_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the President",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nThe Executive Assistant to the President provides high-level, strategic, and confidential support to the President and manages the day-to-day operations of the Office of the President. Serving as a trusted member of the team and representative of the President’s Office, the Executive Assistant exercises exceptional judgment, discretion, diplomacy, and initiative in managing the President’s time, priorities, relationships, communications, and engagements. The Executive Assistant serves as a key liaison among the President, Board of Trustees, Cabinet and Leadership Team, faculty, staff, students, alumni, donors, parents, community leaders, and other constituents. The position works closely with the Chief of Staff and senior leaders to advance presidential and institutional priorities and ensure effective coordination and follow-through. Essential Responsibilities Executive Support and Office Management Serve as a trusted executive assistant to the President, anticipating needs, managing competing priorities, and ensuring timely follow-through. Manage the daily operations of the Office of the President, including systems, records, budgets, correspondence, and special projects. Serve as a primary point of contact and liaison for the President, representing the Office with high levels of professionalism, warmth, discretion, and sound judgment. Partner with the Chief of Staff to coordinate presidential priorities, communications, meetings, projects, and institutional initiatives. Track commitments, decisions, and action items and ensure appropriate follow-up. Handle highly confidential personnel, governance, legal, donor, student, and institutional matters with discretion. Calendar, Travel, and Presidential Readiness Manage the President’s complex calendar, prioritizing requests and ensuring effective use of the President’s time. Serve as a thoughtful gatekeeper, determining when matters require presidential attention and when they can be delegated or redirected. Prepare agendas, briefing materials, talking points, background information, and schedules for meetings and engagements. Coordinate domestic and international travel, including itineraries, logistics, expenses, and reimbursements. Communications and Governance Manage presidential correspondence and draft, edit, and proofread communications in the President’s voice. Provide administrative and logistical support for the President’s work with the Board of Trustees and its committees, in partnership with the Secretary of the College. Support preparation for Board, Cabinet, Leadership Team, and presidential committee meetings. Events and External Engagement Coordinate presidential and College-wide events, formal protocol events, and high-profile engagements. Partner with Communications and Marketing, Advancement, Academic Affairs, Student Affairs, Facilities, and other offices to ensure successful events and engagements. Meet regularly and communicate effectively with the President’s spouse, who plays a key role in supporting the work of the College, particularly events sponsored by the President’s Office Support the President’s relationships with trustees, donors, alumni, parents, community leaders, government officials, civic organizations, and other strategic constituents. Support presidential activities related to fundraising, stewardship, strategic planning, community relations, and other institutional priorities. Other Responsibilities Monitor the Office of the President’s operating budget and process expenditures in accordance with College policies. Maintain systems that promote efficiency, accountability, communication, and follow-through. Perform other duties and special projects as assigned by the President. Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required. Seven or more years of progressively responsible experience supporting a senior executive, preferably in higher education, nonprofit organizations, or another complex, collaborative environment. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to draft polished executive correspondence. Outstanding organizational, project management, prioritization, and follow-through skills. Demonstrated ability to exercise independent judgment, anticipate needs, solve problems, and manage competing priorities. Exceptional discretion and ability to handle confidential and sensitive information with integrity. Strong interpersonal skills, emotional intelligence, diplomacy, and a calm, welcoming demeanor. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office 365, including Outlook, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Teams. Experience in higher education and familiarity with the operations and governance of a liberal arts college strongly preferred. Working Conditions This is an exempt position requiring a strong on-campus presence and flexibility to support the priorities of the Office of the President. Evening and weekend work is occasionally required for College events, presidential engagements, Board meetings, commencement, and other institutional activities. Application To apply, please submit a cover letter and resume through the College’s employment portal. Full benefits information including eligibility details is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203164699348_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant Career Advancement and Community Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nThe Executive Assistant for Career Advancement and Community Engagement provides multifaceted administrative and project support in the areas of career advancement and community outreach and engagement. This role helps ensure seamless execution of key projects and priorities that advance the College’s mission to foster student success and strengthen employer partner and community engagement. The Executive Assistant serves as liaison among faculty, staff, students, alumni, parents, Trustees, community members, employers, and campus partners, handling sensitive information with discretion and professionalism. The nature of this full-time position will include occasional evening and/or weekend work. Key Responsibilities: Providing administrative support and leadership for key projects, priorities, and outreach related to Career Advancement and Community Engagement. Serving as a project/event /budget manager and point of contact for campus, employers, and community partners to help ensure successful execution of assigned priorities.  Researching and compiling information, reports, and data to support strategic decision-making and programming as it relates to the areas mentioned above. Assisting in planning, promoting, and coordinating career advancement, networking, and community engagement opportunities for students, alumni, and community partners.  Helping develop partnerships with local businesses, nonprofits, and educational institutions to expand student internship and college engagement opportunities. Building and maintaining relationships with campus partners and college stakeholders (students, alumni, parents, Trustees, employers, and community members). Representing Lake Forest College values in all communications and interactions. Administrative & Operational Support: Preparing and editing correspondence, contact reports, meeting agendas, and presentations with accuracy and attention to detail. Managing calendars, scheduling meetings, and coordinating travel arrangements Maintaining organized electronic budget and filing systems and processing invoices and expense reports  Developing programming, event marketing, and social media content Collaborating with campus and community partner marketing and communications teams to promote events, partnerships, and student success stories.  Meeting & Event Coordination: Organizing and preparing materials for meetings, conferences, and campus or community events. Recording meeting minutes, tracking follow-up actions, and ensuring timely completion of project and priorities.  Coordinating logistics for events hosted by Career Advancement and Community Engagement teams. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience is required. At least two years of related administrative experience is required. Prior experience in a similar role in higher education and career services or community engagement is preferred. Candidates must possess excellent written and verbal communication skills, be a self-starter, have strong organizational, detail-orientation, and time management skills, and be able to proactively manage multiple tasks and meet assigned deadlines. The successful candidate must also be proficient in Microsoft Office Suite, and be comfortable learning new software, working with data, CRM systems, and various social media platforms. The ability to interact positively and effectively with college stakeholders is an integral part of this role.  Full benefits information including eligibility details is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203133264954_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Coach",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nLake Forest College, located just north of Chicago, invites applications for the anticipated part-time stipend positions for Assistant Coaches in the following sports: Football, Men’s Volleyball, Women’s Volleyball, Men’s Football (Defense), Men’s Basketball, Women’s Basketball, Women’s Soccer, Men’s Soccer, Women’s Golf, Men’s Lacrosse, Cheerleading, Dance Team, Track and Field. These roles offer an excellent opportunity for individuals seeking experience in a Division III collegiate coaching environment. The position requires a significant commitment to evening and weekend work. The ideal candidate will demonstrate the ability to work collaboratively within the Forester athletic department, supporting the mission, goals, and procedures of both the department and the College. Key Responsibilities:  Responsibilities include assisting the head coach with practice and game preparation, recruiting and developing student-athletes, preparing scouting reports, and performing other duties as assigned by the Head Sport Coach. The successful candidate will foster a positive team environment and motivate student-athletes. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s Degree Collegiate playing and/or coaching experience preferred. Candidates must possess strong interpersonal and communication skills, and the ability to work in a team environment. Salary and Benefits:  Salary:  Salary is a seasonal stipend that varies based on sport and team available budget. The range per sport: Football (4,500 - 5,500), Men’s and Women’s Volleyball ($10,000-$15,000), Men’s and Women’s Basketball ($15,000-$24,000), Women’s Soccer ($3,000-$6,000), Men’s Soccer ($8,000-$15,000) Cheerleading and Dance ($8,000-$10,000), Women’s Golf ($5,000-$7,000), Men’s Lacrosse ($10,000-$15,000), Track and Field (3,000-6,000), Sports Information (8,000-10,000) depending on position. Sport head coach will discuss salary available if you are selected for a phone interview. Benefits information is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203162261155_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Cheerleading Coach",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nLake Forest College, located just north of Chicago, invites applications for the anticipated part-time stipend positions for Assistant Coaches in the following sport: Cheerleading and Dance. The role offers an excellent opportunity for individuals seeking experience in a Division III collegiate coaching environment. The position requires a significant commitment to evening and weekend work. The ideal candidate will demonstrate the ability to work collaboratively within the Forester athletic department, supporting the mission, goals, and procedures of both the department and the College.   Key Responsibilities:  Responsibilities include assisting the head coach with practice and game preparation, recruiting and developing student-athletes, preparing scouting reports, and performing other duties as assigned by the Head Sport Coach. The successful candidate will foster a positive team environment and motivate student-athletes.   Required Qualifications Bachelor’s Degree Collegiate playing and/or coaching experience preferred. Candidates must possess strong interpersonal and communication skills, and the ability to work in a team environment.   Salary and Benefits:  Salary:  Salary is a seasonal stipend that varies based on sport and team available budget. The range per sport: Cheerleading and Dance ($8,000-$10,000). Benefits information is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203156218598_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor American Public Law and Politics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nThe Lake Forest College Department of Politics and International Relations invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor specializing in American public law and politics to begin in August 2027. We seek someone to teach Introduction to American Politics and Introduction to Legal Studies, as well as intermediate- and advanced-level courses with an American public law focus. The Department presently offers a rich variety of courses in American constitutional law and criminal law. While we encourage candidates of all substantive interests to apply, we are especially interested in someone who can further broaden the Department’s specialized public law courses in areas other than criminal law. The successful candidate will contribute to the course offerings of the very popular Legal Studies minor and assist in the activities of the Mock Trial program. Candidates with experience in conducting Mock Trial , who can connect students with legal internships and opportunities in the Chicagoland area, or who can support the Public Policy Studies minor – such as by teaching courses at the intersections of American public law, politics, and public policy at the local, state, and national levels of government in the United States – are strongly encouraged to apply.    A Ph.D. in political science or a related field and/or a J.D. is required upon the start of the 2027-28 academic year. The teaching load is six courses across two academic semesters. The successful candidate will typically have five course preparations a year, with one course – Introduction to American Politics – offered twice and can expect a rotation of courses that will also include Introduction to Legal Studies, American Constitutional Law, the American Judiciary, as well as public law courses in their areas of expertise. They must also be willing to teach a course in the College's first-year studies program, including while on the tenure track. Class enrollments at Lake Forest College are small, typically between 15 to 25 students. We seek someone who is dedicated to building a career centered on teaching and advising excellence.   We expect candidates to show scholarly promise and have a robust and active research program, which would need to include peer-reviewed publications at the time of tenure. Lake Forest College supports faculty research through the provision of student research assistants and the potential to apply for summer research funds. The successful passage of the third-year review earns a one-semester sabbatical at full pay in the fourth or fifth year of service. Tenure review takes place at the start of the sixth year. The Department is especially interested in candidates who can incorporate students into their research programs. To this end, Lake Forest College’s proximity to Chicago provides an opportunity to use the city as a “laboratory” for student learning.   Applicants should submit, in a single PDF , a curriculum vitae, an unofficial graduate transcript, a teaching statement that addresses diversity in a liberal arts setting, sample syllabi, a single sample of scholarly writing, three confidential letters of recommendation, and evidence of teaching effectiveness . Confidential letters of recommendation should be emailed to  politicssearch2026@lakeforest.edu . Other materials should be uploaded to the  Lake Forest College Career Center website . Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, but they must be received by October 15, 2026, to be assured full consideration.    A selective liberal arts college located on Chicago’s North Shore, Lake Forest College enrolls approximately 1,800 students from more than 40 states and from more than 100 countries. Lake Forest is one of the most diverse small colleges in the Midwest with approximately 40% of our student body comprised of domestic students of color and international students. For three years running (2023, 2024, and 2025), U.S. News & World Report rated Lake Forest College among the top three national liberal arts colleges for social mobility, which measures a college’s success in graduating students from disadvantaged socio-economic backgrounds and transitioning them into the working world. In 2023, U.S. News also ranked Lake Forest the nation’s thirteenth “Best Value” national liberal arts college. In December 2023, The Wall Street Journal rated Lake Forest the top liberal arts college in the Midwest. At Lake Forest College, the quality of a faculty member’s teaching is the most important criterion for evaluation.  Applications will be accepted until the position is filled, but they must be received by October 15, 2026, to be assured full consideration  Compensation and Benefits: The salary offered for this position is: $67,000 -$71,000. Benefit information is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits   If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203149759771_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Head Handball Coach",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-20\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nLake Forest College, located just north of Chicago, invites applications for the part-time position of  Head Handball Coach . This role provides a comprehensive opportunity for an individual passionate about working in collegiate athletics. Coaching our 51x National Championships program with extremely talented student-athletes within a highly selective liberal arts environment are highlights of the role. The position requires a strong on-campus presence, including significant evenings and weekends, and reports to the Assistant Vice President for Athletics. We encourage applicants to submit a cover letter and resume demonstrating the requisite experience for the role. Key Responsibilities:  Organize all facets of the Program:  Scheduling, travel, team roster management, departmental responsibilities for budget reconciliation, practice planning, and team academic monitoring. Recruiting:  Recruit qualified student-athletes for both the men’s and women’s programs at the College. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s Degree 1-3 years of experience in collegiate or professional athletic experience in lacrosse Excellent time management and communication skills Ability to work collaboratively within a team-oriented, fast-paced environment while managing multiple priorities. Salary: $17,000- $20,000 Benefits information is located at:  www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit  Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=42c57bd5-6043-4b07-a424-fa124627e8d0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203146322420_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-13\n\nLocation: Lake Forest\n\nThe Lake Forest College Biology Department invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level, starting August 2027. We are seeking a highly qualified biologist to help lead our introductory biology course BIOL 120: Organismal Biology and teach our upper-level Plant Biology course BIOL 384, both with laboratories. There is also the opportunity to develop further courses in the applicant’s area of expertise, including teaching in the college-wide First Year Studies Program.   Our introductory organismal biology course embraces the diversity of biological systems and seeks to integrate topics from molecules to ecosystems in terms of understanding organisms. As a department, we value inquiry-based learning in our lab courses and we incorporate scientific literature starting in first-year courses. The Biology Department actively seeks candidates whose professional and lived experiences strengthen their ability to teach and mentor all of our students, who embody a variety of identities, experiences, and preparations.  Successful candidates will express enthusiasm for a liberal arts education. Postdoctoral experience is required and demonstrated excellence in teaching beyond the traditional TA experience is strongly preferred. This position involves teaching three courses a semester (labs count as a full course) and developing an active undergraduate-led research program that expands our departmental expertise and that results in both peer-reviewed publications and efforts to secure external funding. Tenure-track faculty at Lake Forest College who pass their third-year review will earn a half-year sabbatical at full pay, in preparation for tenure. Lake Forest College has been recognized as a top college by U.S. News & World Report, including #1 in the nation for Social Mobility, #13 as a Best Value College. The Wall Street Journal rated the College as the #1 Liberal Arts College in the Midwest, while the Princeton Review ranks the College #2 for best science lab facilities (including a research-grade greenhouse, tissue culture, microscopy, imaging facilities, and aquatic and terrestrial vivaria), among other top distinctions. Support for undergraduate researchers, federally-funded faculty, and a full-time lab manager make our department thrive. Members of the Biology Department actively engage in interdisciplinary programs in Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Environmental Studies, and Neuroscience, and we welcome candidates with interdisciplinary teaching and research interests.  A selective liberal arts college located on Chicago’s North Shore, Lake Forest College enrolls approximately 1,800 students from more than 40 states and from more than 100 countries. Lake Forest College is one of the most diverse small colleges in the Midwest with nearly 40% of our student body comprised of domestic students of color and international students. The majority of our STEM student pool is female and includes a significant number of first generation and traditionally underrepresented students. At Lake Forest College, the quality of a faculty member’s teaching is the most important criterion for evaluation. The College also expects peer-reviewed publications and active participation in the College community. If you are interested in learning more about employment at Lake Forest College, please visit Working at Lake Forest | Lake Forest College In a single PDF, please upload the following: 1) a cover letter that describes your motivation for applying to Lake Forest College, 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) a statement of teaching philosophy highlighting your approach to Organismal Biology (BIOL 120) and Plant Biology (BIOL 384), 4) a statement highlighting how you will build a 5-year program designed around undergraduate research, 5) a statement that describes concrete strategies you would use to teach and support all our students, who have a wide range of experiences and preparations, throughout their college experience, and 6) representative (p)reprints. Please provide names and contact information for three recommenders. Questions may be addressed to Associate Professor Rebecca Delventhal, biosearch@lakeforest.edu .  Review of applications will begin on September 21, 2026 Compensation and Benefits: The salary offered for this position is: $67,000 -$74,000. Benefit information is located at: www.lakeforest.edu/offices-and-departments/human-resources/employee-benefits",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
